  "detail": "`adoptWorkerCommit` ends with `resetTo(cwd, sha)`, which runs `git reset --hard` and then `git clean -fdq`, and doesn't take the signal. If the reset succeeds and the clean then fails (for example a confinement or preparation failure), the helper throws while HEAD already points to the worker commit, which breaks its \"HEAD unchanged on refusal\" contract. A cancel during that step is also ignored. Adoption has already verified that the worktree is clean, untracked files included, so the clean step is unnecessary there. Required: end adoption with one `worktreeGit([\"git\", \"reset\", \"--hard\", \"-q\", sha], { cwd, signal })` instead of `resetTo`, and leave `resetTo` unchanged for its other callers. Test: an injected failure of any step after the checks either leaves HEAD at the parent or the helper succeeds; a cancel before the reset leaves HEAD at the parent. No change is needed for writers racing adoption: the run's own engine is the only writer of its worktree, and it is awaiting the adoption."
